  "summary_md": "A wave of portfolio reshaping—via divestitures, acquisitions, and outright buyouts—is realigning the specialty and generic drug landscape. CPIX is exiting its branded business to become a pure-play orphan developer, while ASRT awaits a Garda Therapeutics buyout that both floors and caps its value. The sharpest dislocation is at ORGO, where a sudden CMS reimbursement change caused a dramatic demand collapse and credibility crisis, contrasting with IRWD’s standout momentum: robust LINZESS demand, raised guidance, and an imminent pediatric label decision are driving a stock rally.\n\nAcross the batch, capital access remains a defining theme, with AQST securing non-dilutive debt while BTAI and SNOA lean on dilutive equity raises. Many managements are pursuing cost discipline to reach profitability (EOLS, ANIK), and early-stage pipelines are progressing toward catalysts (BIOA, SCYX, TLPH). The stark divide between accelerating launches like DERM’s AAMROCI and sudden regulatory fractures at ORGO underscores how single-product or policy exposures can rapidly rewrite stories in this group.",
